abel equals  chunk a so instead of repeating all the  chunk options from chunk a you simply  set a single option ops.label equals  chunk a and then chunk B will use the  same chunk options as chunk a then if  you want to reuse  um both chunk options and the content  from other code chunks you can you can  set the chunk option ref not label to  the label of other code chunks and then  wrap the labels in the function I for  example here I have chunk a which has a  few chunk options and then it also has  some chunk content and then in chunk B I  said ref dot label equals I chunk a that  means I want to use both the content of  Chunk a and the chunk options of Chunk a  so in that way you don't have to repeat  everything from chunk a you simply set a  single option and Knitter will copy  everything to the next to to chunk B so  I also want to share two quick tips  first the chunk options ref not label  and Ops dot label can take a vector of  Chunk labels that means you can reuse a  multiple code chunks instead of a single  one  and second you can  provide further local chunk options if  you if a code chunk has some local chunk  options they will override the reused  options of the same names so in the in  this example chunk B is basically  reusing both chunk options in the chunk  content of Chunk a but it has set a  local chunk option Dev equals PNG  so since we have borrowed all the chunk  options from chunk a which uh includes  the chunk option Dev equals SVG so now  chunk B since chunk B has a local chunk  option W equals PNG this depth equals  PNG will overwrite the dev equals SVG  from chunk a  so basically  local chunk options will have higher  priorities  if you reuse chunk options from other  codes chunks third thing I want to  mention is some the new chunk options in  Knitter in recent years we have added a  few of them and but today I'm just going  to mention one of them here which is the  file option so sometimes you might you  may want to develop code in in an  external script and include it in a code  chunk previously what you could do is to  use the chunk option code so for example  here I'm using the chunk option code  that takes the value from read lines  script.r so that basically means that I  want to read an external script and pass  the content of that script to the code  option and then enter nodes okay I will  use the content of that script as the  content of this code chunk  that that's the previous way of  including external scripts into a code  chunk now this can be simplified to  the chunk option file so you can set  file equals a file path for example  script.r so basically that's equivalent  to using Code equals read lines  script.r before and this file option  also supports a vector of file paths for  example we can pass two scripts to the  file option and the file option will  read both of them into the code chunk  the fourth thing I want to highlight is  some new engines initer the first one is  comment engine so you can use the  comment engine to comment out any  content  and the content can contain basically  anything including code chunks or  include or inline R expressions or other  paragraphs or anything so basically  there's one rule that you have to  remember so if the comment contains  like three tactics and then for the  comment engine  you have to start with four backticks so  basically the rule is that if you're if  your comment contains n tactics then the  comment engine  needs to start with M plus one backtics  so one if the content is commented out  it will it it will not be included in  the output and the the second engine is  the verbatim engine and this can be very  useful when you want to write tutorials  on our markdown so and and you may want  to display some content verbatim  especially the codes chunks when you  want to display a code chunk verbatim  you can use the verbatim engine  and the rule is the same so for example  here I'm including a code chunk with  three back clicks and then the repeating  engine needs to start with four back  ticks for the verbatim engine depending  on the content of the  depending on the language of the content  you can you can set a syntax  highlighting language through the chunk  option Lan so for example here I'm using  Lan equals markdown that means okay the  verbatim content here is markdown code  but this Lang option option is optional  so it depends on whether you want to  syntax highlighting in your output for  the repeating content  and previously I have introduced some  old  ugly hacks  on how to show content repeating and now  you can forget all of them and here um I  just yeah I I don't want to explain this  anymore this is just too ugly use the  verbatim engine and forget about the old  ugly hacks  third engine that I want to show is the  embed engine you can use it's the you  can use the embed engine to embed  external files text files actually  you can embed external text files and  display their content repeat him so this  is similar to the verbatim engine but  you can use the fire option to  uh embed external files  so you don't have to write the content  in the in the chunk body you just point  the file option to some file paths and  alternatively you can write file paths  in the chunk body of the embed engine  for example you can write quote foo.r  code bar dot r  so the second way has a tiny advantage  that is if you write in the chunk body  if you write the file paths in the chunk  body your editor for example R Studio  may be able to out complete the file  paths the last engine that I want to  show here is the exact engine so this  engine  allows you to execute an arbitrary  system command on a file that contains  the content of the chunk  so that command can be specified through  the chunk option command so for example  I have an exact  engine  here and I'm using the command equals  bash that means I want to run the  command bash on  the the command that I specify  in the chunk body  so basically when when Nature sees  an exact  chunk it will write the chunk body into  a file and execute the command on that  file  so for this batch example actually you  don't have to use the exact engine you  can simply use the existing batch engine  in Knitter so these two these two cut  chunks are pretty much equivalent  there have existed a lot of  command-based engines in Knitter for  example Arc or go or Pearl or Z shell  and from now on we no longer need to add  a new engine for every single command  you can basically use the exact engine  to execute any command  and by the way the exact  engine is also extensible so you can if  you're interested you can go back and  check out the examples example on the  link through the link here so for those  who are curious about how the exact  engine works so basically uh  network will execute  a command of the form command arcs1 rx2  are sorry command args one arcs then  args2 where RX is a file path and this  file contains the content of the code  chunk and August 1 and rx2 are optional  command line arguments that you might  you may specify through the chunk option  engine.ops so here I have an example  that executes the grep command  uh with a few command line arguments  Dash I and which means case insensitive  matching and then the pattern is hhe  and I want to  execute grep on the four lines of text  here so you know h e will if you match h  e in case insensitivity it will match  the first line hello and the third line  since it has the which contains h e  and just a quick recap we have uh talked  about four things in today's talk first  thing is that you can use Hash Pipe  comments in the chunk body to write  chunk options you can then you can use  upstart label and ref not label options  to reuse both chunk options and content  and third you can use the file option to  read external files as the chunk content  and last you we have introduced a few  new engines  in knitter so that's all I have for you  today thank you very much and happy  knitting


